{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.046,
            0.346,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.088,
            0.707,
            0.111
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Perfect Cubes and Consecutive Odd Numbers"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.12,
            0.848,
            0.159
          ],
          "content": "Consecutive odd numbers have a role to play with cubes too. Look at the following pattern:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.464,
            0.161,
            0.56,
            0.181
          ],
          "content": "1 = 1 = 1 ^ { 3 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.447,
            0.191,
            0.576,
            0.211
          ],
          "content": "3+5=8=2^{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.42,
            0.221,
            0.604,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "7+9+11=27=3^{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.387,
            0.251,
            0.638,
            0.271
          ],
          "content": "13+15+17+19=64=4^{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.358,
            0.281,
            0.665,
            0.301
          ],
          "content": "21+23+25+27+29=125=5^{3}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_7.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.311,
            0.689,
            0.331
          ],
          "content": "31+33+35+37+39+41=216=6^{3}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.336,
            0.817,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "Later in this series, we get the following set of consecutive numbers:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.251,
            0.36,
            0.746,
            0.38
          ],
          "content": "91+93+95+97+99+101+103+105+107+109.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.39,
            0.735,
            0.412
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Can you tell what this sum is without doing the calculation?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.429,
            0.295,
            0.451
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Cube Roots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.46,
            0.352,
            0.48
          ],
          "content": "We know that $8 = 2 ^ { 3 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.485,
            0.672,
            0.506
          ],
          "content": "We call 2 the cube root of 8 and denote this by $2 \\equiv \\sqrt [ 3 ] { 8 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.509,
            0.846,
            0.554
          ],
          "content": "More generally, if $y = x ^ { 3 } ,$ then x is the cube root of y. This is denoted by $x = \\sqrt[3]{y} . \\mathrm{SO}, \\sqrt[3]{8} = \\sqrt[3]{2^3} = 2.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.556,
            0.751,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": "Similarly, $\\sqrt[3]{27} = \\sqrt[3]{3^3} = 3$ and $\\sqrt[3]{1000} = \\sqrt[3]{10^{3}} = 3;$ . In general, $\\sqrt[3]{n^{3}} = n$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.578,
            0.847,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": "How do we find out if a number is a cube? Taking inspiration from the case of squares, let us see if we can use prime factorisations."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.625,
            0.514,
            0.648
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Let us check if 3375 is a perfect cube."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.369,
            0.65,
            0.628,
            0.669
          ],
          "content": "3375 = 3 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 5.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.675,
            0.847,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "Can the factors be split into three identical groups? For 3375, we can form three groups of $(3 \\times 5)$ . So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.363,
            0.712,
            0.658,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "\\begin{array} { r l } & { 3 3 7 5 = ( 3 \\times 5 ) \\times ( 3 \\times 5 ) \\times ( 3 \\times 5 ) } \\\\ & { \\quad = ( 3 \\times 5 ) ^ { 3 } = 1 5 ^ { 3 } . } \\end{array}",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_21.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.754,
            0.831,
            0.792
          ],
          "content": "Another way is to check if the factors can be grouped into triplet(s): $3 3 7 5 = ( \\overline { 3 } \\times \\overline { 3 \\times 3 } ) \\times ( 5 \\times 5 \\times 5 ) = 3 ^ { 3 } \\times 5 ^ { 3 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.793,
            0.377,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": "This means $\\sqrt[3]{3375} = 15$"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.822,
            0.367,
            0.844
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "？ Is 500 a perfect cube?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.847,
            0.847,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": "50 $2 \\times 2 \\times 5 \\times 5 \\times 5$ . We see that the factors cannot be split into three identical groups. Therefore, 500 is not a perfect cube."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.086,
            0.915,
            0.119,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "1 4"
        },
        {
